Subject: Reply to Martin R. Olah

>From: martino@gomez.Jpl.Nasa.Gov (Martin R. Olah)
>
>Regarding the Tim Starr essay, I am not familiar with the Parental Choice
>in Education Amendment but have some general comments anyway. The main reason
>a private education may be superior to public is parental attitude. A parent
>paying for an otherwise free education will want to see some results. I spent
>9 years in Catholic schools and was not taught to think for myself or question
>authority anymore than in a public institution. But my parents did insist that
>my grades be kept up or they would send me to public school (an effective
>threat). 

The point that parental involvement in their child's education is a good
one, but there's a difference between public school and Catholic school
that's relevant: better teaching method.  For 35 years, the Catholic schools
have been using the phonic method of teaching reading.  The public schools
have, by and large, been using the "look-say" method, which amounts to
teaching dyslexia.

Is there anyone on this list who learned to read via the look-say method?
A.K.A. the "hieroglyphic" method, or the "look-your-guess-is-as-good-as-mine"
method?  John Dewey, the closest thing there is to a patron saint of public
school, knew that this method caused illiteracy, and supported it for that
very reason.  Dewey opposed development of the individual mind because it
didn't fit in with his collectivist ideals.

>People who believe that it is only the school that makes the difference
>are quick to suggest government-supplied vouchers. This could damage education
>as much as medicare hurt healthcare in this country.  Private education costs
>could inflate in order to absorb all of the new funds available.  The 
>government would also have an excuse to further control the curricula of 
>these once private schools. As many of us would agree, government and
>private partnerships can be a recipe for disaster.

The comparison to medicare and medicaid is a disanalogy.  Both socialized
medical insurance programs pay the supplier directly, rather than the
patients.  In order to make it analogous, they would have to operate by
issuing vouchers to everyone eligible for the program which the patients
could then spend on the medical care of their choice from the supplier of
their choice.  This would be a great improvement over the present system,
and, combined with Medisave accounts, would enable financial support
support for the medical care of the needy to be privatized.

As for the "inflation," do food stamps "inflate" the price of food?

The big risk is the regulation, and the CA amendment has language to the
effect that the current ones would be frozen, new regulations for the
protection of health and safety only would have to apply universally to
the public and voucher schools, and the burden of proof that they are
needed would be put on the State, rather than putting the burden that they
are unnecessary on the voucher schools.  Manny Klausner wrote this part
of the amendment.

