Date: Fri, 2 Jul 1993 05:12:06 -0700 (PDT)
From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Critical Bits, Structure, and Information

Hal Finney:

>The introduction of fitness landscapes, and stipulations that the objects
>under study (organisms, or Go programs) have to have a certain amount of 
>fitness in competition with others, add a great deal of intellectual baggage
>to an otherwise clean theory.

Or we could say that AIT adds baggage to an otherwise clean GA theory!
Actually, neither one are "clean": AIT can't actually find the minimum
size program in the general case, and GA can't find the optimum program
in the general case.  AIT radically changes the meanings of not one but
three perfectly good English words ("information", "complexity", and 
"random") to describe one theoretical concept, causing those terms to be
misapplied in contexts where the original meanings would be more
appropriate.  GA is not free of adding semantic baggage either, eg 
subtly changing the evolution theory definition of "fitness".

Editing distance and crossover distance between 
points in a schema space are entirely objective.  Subjective "fitness" 
is only needed in computing multi-step editing probability, since the 
probability of each step is modified by the fitness function.  

>We may agree that in "pure" AIT a blended frog (one whose molecules have been
>given random positions) has greater information content (at the molecular
>level) than a living frog.  

Only wrt the context-free definition of "information" in AIT, which
is quite different from the normal meaning of "information".
(This needs to be clarified often, especially in a learning
seminar, or confusion will soon reign).

Let's combine editing distance and AIT to resolve the frog paradox.

Editing involves destroying patterns as well as expanding,
compressing, or copying them.  This models a world in
which patterns change quickly, but the underlying amount of 
memory changes much more slowly, necessitating the destruction
of many patterns.

Let's compare the algorithm needed to "edit" the frog from
living state to soup state, and vice versa.  To get from frog
to soup, we can recursively chop the frog in half, a very short 
algorithm.  To go from soup to frog, we must at least (assuming 
the DNA is intact) go through the laborious process of inserting the
DNA in a viable zygote, then incubate the entire embryological
process.  If we don't have the DNA it's even worse; we might have
to evolve the frog from scratch.  The "algorithms" needed to
"edit" soup into frog depends on complex patterns
in the environment, and are therefore quite long.  They also
take longer to accomplish the edit, they are "logically deep".
The frog is more organized than frog soup; it is more difficult
to achieve.

The frog-to-soup algorithm destroys
patterns, thus lies outside the explanatory scope of 
present AIT.  The soup-to-frog algorithms might be described, 
in theory, by AIT, but not without invoking environmental 
data patterns.  Furthermore, both embryology and evolution involve 
destruction of patterns which isn't be modelled by AIT.

More rigorous than "editing" frogs is editing poker decks.
A card deck at the end of the game, after then final hands 
are gathered but before the deck is shuffled, is far more
organized than after the deck is shuffled.  The algorithm
to get from shuffled deck to pre-shuffled deck is quite
AIT-complex (game rules and strategies) and logically deep
(thousands of discards, etc. based on working through 
strategy algorithms) while three or four simple shuffles
destroys that complex and deep pattern, returning the
the deck to a high state of randomness.  

We can also trade off AIT-complexity with logical depth, for
example by starting off with just the poker rules and evolving the
strategies with GA.  Interestingly, the first game's
finished-hands deck will be as random as the shuffled deck,
but the finished-hands deck organization will increase with 
log(number of games played).

In these two examples, we have a finite "memory": the frog
material and the poker deck, thus the need to destroy patterns.
Another example: fixed-memory algorithmic editing distance from 
one pseudorandom sequence to another may be minimally described by 
the algorithm "destroy the first sequence and generate another".  

Two simple editing algorithms are altering one bit (mutation)
and cutting & pasting bit patterns (crossover).  GA schema
theory usually restricts itself to these two algorithms.
These two "genetic operators" also provide a common measure
of editing distance.   AIT cannot provide a common measure
(because the minimal editing algorithm is undecidable), but
it is more general than mutation & crossover.

> [Chaitin] argues that one region of a 
> living creature (one cell of a frog, say) can be used to predict 
> the structure of another region (another cell) with a reduced p
> program size in the AIT sense.

But the same is true of crystals, photons, hydrogen atoms, 
orbits, unshattered proteins and DNA in the blendered frog,
etc.  Compressibility, redundancy, symmetry, etc. are
not defining characteristics of life.  Neither is self-reproduction
of an information pattern for that matter; many chemical reactions
do that, including crystals some of which replicate fairly complex
patterns millions of times over. 

