~  SPECIAL KEYS

      Certain keys on your terminal or PC keyboard serve special roles in
a market session.  They are as follows:

~  Esc (Cancel)  --  Pressing the "Esc" key while a menu is displayed will
      return you to the previous menu.  Pressing the "Esc" key while an
      action is pending (i.e. while the system is waiting for you to
      provide information necessary for some action) will cancel the
      current action and return you to the previous menu. 
      Note:  When logins are made via TELNET or modem, it may be necessary
      to press the "Esc" key twice in succession to cancel a pending
      operation.

~  ? (Help)  --  This is the help key.  From almost any point in a market
      session, pressing this key will cause helpful information on the
      current menu or activity to appear on screen.  Pressing the "Esc" key
      will return you to the current menu or activity.

~   and  (Up and Down Cursor Keys)  --  These two cursor keys move the
      highlight bar in the Menu Window up or down one line at a time.  You
      may use these cursor keys to select an option and then press the
      "enter" key to initiate that option.  (Alternatively, you can select
      and initiate an option by pressing the option letter, even if the
      desired option is not highlighted.)
      Note:  The Up and Down Cursor Keys may not function properly on some
      terminals or terminal emulators.

~  +, -, Pgup, Pgdn (Item Selection Keys) -- Use these keys to move the
      highlight bar in the Market Window.  Since the highlighted contract
      is taken as the default for options selected from the Menu Window,
      preselecting a contract with the Item Selection Keys will simplify
      subsequent market actions.
      Note:  On some terminals or terminal emulators, the - and + keys on
      the numeric keypad may not function as described here, but the - and
      + keys on the top row of the keyboard should work properly.

~  , , Backspace (Entry Editing Keys)  --  Use these keys to edit items
      you type in from the keyboard.



